WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.210
Before counting anything, ask one question:

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.430
does order matter?

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.420
Choosing a winner and a runner-up is different from

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.670
choosing a team of two.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.560
On the podium, swapping the two people

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.550
changes the result.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.250
In a team, swapping them changes nothing.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.680
We divide by two because each team was counted

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.790
in two orders.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.930
The number you divide by is the number of ways to

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.240
arrange the chosen objects.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.370
Three runners, Ava, Ben and Cal, are in a race.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.730
In how many ways can first and second place be filled?

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.720
And how many different pairs of them could form a team?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.090
Pause and try both.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.660
For the podium, there are three choices for first place,

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.940
then two remaining choices for second.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.880
Three times two is six ordered results.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.920
For a team, Ava then Ben is the same team as

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:54.890
Ben then Ava.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.330
The six ordered results merge in pairs,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.670
leaving three teams.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.030
Now choose three people from five.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.270
In order, that is five times four times three,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:09.970
which is sixty.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.720
As an unordered group, each set of three people can be

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.980
arranged in six orders, so sixty divided by six

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.130
gives ten groups.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:24.980
Read the wording carefully.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.800
If the three chosen people get different jobs,

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.600
such as captain, scorer and timekeeper,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.840
then order matters again and the answer is sixty.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.350
If positions or roles differ, count in order.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.620
If only membership matters, divide by the number of

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.230
arrangements of the chosen group.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.340
Notebook challenge: from six players, how many ways are

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.490
there to choose a captain and a vice-captain?

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.360
And how many ways to choose two players to

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.250
carry the equipment?
